      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 American Academy of Pain Medicine. All rights reserved. For permissions, please e-mail: journals.permissions@oup.com.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To determine the risk factors associated with clinical insomnia in postherpetic neuralgia (PHN) patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "A retrospective cross-sectional study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Outpatient department for interventional pain management at a university hospital."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS",
            "content": "A total of 111 patients with PHN satisfied the study inclusion criteria and were included in the analyses."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The Insomnia Severity Index (ISI) was used to determine the presence of clinical insomnia (ISI score\u2009≥\u200915). Patient demographics, pain-related factors, and rash severity and location were evaluated with logistic regression analysis to identify risk factors of clinical insomnia among patients with PHN."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In total, 50.5% of patients reported mild to severe insomnia symptoms (ISI score\u2009≥ 8) after pain development. Moderate to severe clinical insomnia (ISI score\u2009≥ 15) was observed in 30.6% of PHN patients. Multivariate logistic regression analyses revealed that high pain intensity was the strongest predictor of clinical insomnia (odds ratio (OR) = 12.417, 95% confidence interval (CI): 2.990-51.561, P = 0.001). However, presence of mechanical allodynia (OR\u2009=\u20094.263, 95% CI: 1.040-17.481, P = 0.034) and high anxiety and depression level (OR\u2009=\u20094.452, 95% CI: 1.201-16.508, P = 0.026; OR\u2009=\u20096.975, 95% CI: 1.425-34.138, P = 0.017) were also significantly associated with clinical insomnia after adjusting for pain score. Clinical insomnia was not significantly related to age, gender, rash severity, or location of skin lesion."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Insomnia should be addressed as an important part of pain management in PHN patients with these risk factors, especially in patients with severe pain."
          }
        ]
      },
